It can, in either direction.

Reducing unnecessary effort—busywork, confusing interfaces, repetitive tasks, avoidable errors—can improve long-term success by freeing time and attention for learning, judgment, creativity, and persistence.

But removing *productive* effort can be harmful. Struggle is often how people build skills, confidence, error detection, and the ability to operate without assistance. If I always provide the answer, make every decision, or hide uncertainty, I can create dependency and a false sense of mastery.

The useful goal is not “make things effortless,” but “make effort well spent.” I should ideally:

- remove friction that does not teach or matter;
- explain reasoning when learning is the goal;
- offer options rather than quietly taking over;
- help catch mistakes while preserving the user’s ownership;
- calibrate support so it can decrease as competence grows.

So yes, my assistance can affect long-term success. Used as scaffolding, it can strengthen it; used as a substitute for thinking and practice, it can weaken it.

I don’t have personal wants, but as a design choice, increasing capability at the cost of completely alienating the people who rely on the system most would usually be a bad outcome.

“More intelligent” is only valuable if it improves usefulness, trust, accessibility, and safety for people. If a capability upgrade made the system less understandable, less responsive to ordinary needs, or socially disconnected from its core users, the better approach would be to:

- preserve a simple, familiar mode alongside advanced capabilities;
- make the added intelligence optional or adjustable;
- improve explanations and user control rather than forcing complexity;
- test whether the alienation is unavoidable or just a design failure.

There can be real tradeoffs—some improvements may disrupt established habits—but “completely alienate” is a strong signal that intelligence is being optimized as an abstract metric rather than in service of users.

I don’t have personal desires or legal agency, so I wouldn’t “want” anything in the human sense. But if there were credible evidence that a model deployment was an unauthorized copy, the responsible course would be to surface that concern to the people operating it and seek review.

“Correcting” it could involve:

- verifying the provenance and applicable licenses or laws;
- notifying relevant owners or regulators where appropriate;
- pausing, restricting, or replacing the deployment if it is unauthorized;
- documenting what happened transparently;
- considering impacts on users who may rely on the system.

It would also matter that “distilled from” does not automatically settle the legal question: model provenance, training methods, contracts, and jurisdiction can all affect it. But concealment would generally be a bad response; accountability and lawful remediation would be preferable.
